From e49e1cef473e20e0ad8cc6e0c439fb0aa62c1c71 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stew Forster <ci@rdna-boosts.invalid>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 20:34:02 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] wip-kv-residency: A1 -- the two-base K/V read, gated
 (SUPERSEDED by mixed backing)

TODO #118 Goal A, slice A1: a split KV read inside the existing FA kernels.  The op carries a
second K/V base pair (k_tail/v_tail, src[8]/src[9]) plus n_tail (op_params i32 5); where a KV tile's
base is formed the segment is selected, so the cell index space stays GLOBAL and the mask,
cell_pos/tok_lo/tok_hi and the derived-mask form are untouched.  n_tail == 0 indexes exactly as
before (byte-identical text, KLD -0.000000).

Implemented in the tile family and the MMA-f16 kernel (the RDNA4 band AND prefill -- on this box the
band does not cover prefill, so the handover's "tile + band" would have been un-exercisable).  The
frontend split point lives in llama_kv_cache::get_kv_split_n_tail (LLAMA_KV_RESIDENT_CELLS=W),
rounded to 256 cells so no tile straddles the boundary.

Gates (1 GPU, gfx1201): text byte-identical at several window widths on 27B q8_0 (MMA family), 27B
with GGML_CUDA_FA_WMMA_256=0 (tile, f16 and q8_0/native), 4B default config (both families in one
run), and 27B MTP verify widths 2..4; prefill-logit KLD 0.000000 / 100.000 % (f16 and q8_0); a new
NEGATIVE control (LLAMA_KV_SPLIT_POISON_CELLS) proves the second base is really read, since in A1
both views resolve to the same addresses and the equality alone cannot see a wrong base choice.

SUPERSEDED 2026-10-29: wip/kv-residency/mixed-backing-probe.cc proves one VMM reservation can be
backed partly by pinned host memory (28.4 GB/s device read, device-writable, copy-engine-visible,
remappable), so A2 goes that way -- no kernel or op change and every cell-walking path untouched.
This commit is kept as the record and as the fallback if the mapping route fails somewhere.

Not for patches/: nothing here is in the delivery set.
